{
  "instrument": {
    "state": "Act on the equipment turnaround process across the four crews, treating each booking as one observation in a time sequence. The reported 19 late starts among 74 bookings establish an aggregate problem, not whether it is persistent, worsening or temporary. Possible contributors include late returns, cleaning delays, inaccurate availability entries and delayed collection; these are hypotheses. Missing entries and separate logs may obscure the sequence.",
    "operation": "During the two-week investigation, link the preceding six weeks of bookings to return, cleaning-completion and collection records by equipment and booking, retaining missing values explicitly. Agree consistent definitions of scheduled start, ready for collection and actual start; do not equate collection with actual start unless that relationship is verified. For every new turnaround, record these events comparably, plus expected return where available, calendar changes and exceptional circumstances. Identify whether equipment was absent, awaiting cleaning or ready but uncollected at the scheduled start; leave ambiguous cases unresolved. Review daily and summarize each week using late bookings divided by all bookings, delay minutes, stage-specific counts and recording completeness. Compare comparable equipment and crew groups before pooling them. Before inspecting the prospective sequence, adopt this provisional persistence rule: a candidate bottleneck must recur on multiple booking days in each investigation week, with the same stage accounting for the greatest documented delay burden in both weeks. At day 14, choose a feasible change aimed at that stage: for example, revise return handoffs, reserve observed turnaround time in the calendar, or introduce a readiness-and-collection handoff. These options remain conditional on findings and must fit existing equipment and staffing. If missing records could reverse the ranking, or bookings are too sparse to apply the rule, choose a standardized recording and handoff trial first rather than claim a bottleneck is established.",
    "signal": "Look for repeated stage-specific delays, consecutive elevated weekly late-start rates, increasing delay duration, reversals, or isolated spikes followed by recovery. Show booking denominators and missing-record counts alongside each time point. A meaningful candidate signal would be a recurring readiness delay despite calendar availability, or repeated late collection after documented readiness. These are observations to seek, not findings already obtained. Historical weeks provide context only where definitions and coverage are comparable.",
    "inference": "Repeated comparable observations can support distinguishing an enduring turnaround constraint from a short-lived disruption and can narrow which operational change deserves a trial. Persistent delay at one stage supports targeting that stage; a rise confined to one equipment type or crew supports a narrower trial. After selection, continue the same measurements through a predefined trial period, preferably staggering introduction across comparable bookings where practical. Sustained improvement across repeated periods would support retaining the change, while isolated improvement would warrant continued observation.",
    "limit": "Timestamp errors, missingness, irregular booking frequency, changing job mix, workload, measurement practices and concurrent interventions can create or hide patterns. Two weeks and small subgroup counts may be insufficient; the persistence rule is a practical selection rule, not statistical proof or causal identification. Test whether plausible assignments of unresolved cases alter the decision. Set trial success and acceptable service-impact thresholds before implementation. Stop or revise a trial if it causes unsafe handoffs or unacceptable disruption; extend observation when evidence is sparse. Check that apparent improvement does not merely shift starts later, suppress bookings or move delays to other crews."
  }
}
